When Pope Gregory IX offered to release Saint Clare from her vow of poverty, she told him she never wanted to be released from following Christ. She was a knight's daughter from Assisi. At about eighteen she left her father's house one night through a door barred with heavy logs and a stone column, and she went to Saint Francis. For forty-two years she lived in one small convent, through nearly thirty years of illness, soldiers inside the walls, and the kind pressure of popes. Why would a woman fight so hard, for so long, to own nothing at all?
